worked_examples.solution:
Fossil-fuel route: the fuel's stored chemical energy is released as heat when it is burned; this heat transfers, by heating, to water, turning it into high-pressure steam;

the steam does mechanical work on a turbine, making it spin; the spinning turbine turns a generator, which supplies electrical energy. Similarity: a nuclear power station reaches the same steam-turbine-generator stage.

Difference: the nuclear station releases its heat through nuclear fission in the reactor core, a nuclear reaction, rather than by burning a fuel, a chemical reaction.
